📄 Report: LLM-Based Approaches for GL Code Prediction
________________________________________
1. Introduction
The objective of this report is to explore different methods for using Large Language Models (LLMs) to predict General Ledger (GL) codes based on invoice data fields such as description, business type, company code, amount, and tax code.
This report outlines multiple solution architectures, their working principles, pros and cons, and recommendations based on project constraints like data volume, accuracy, scalability, and cost.
________________________________________
2. Problem Statement
Goal:
Predict the correct GL Code for a given invoice entry using available invoice fields.
Inputs:
•	Invoice Description
•	Business Type
•	Company Code
•	Amount
•	Tax Code
•	(Optional) Other available fields
Output:
•	Predicted GL Code (numeric or alphanumeric)
________________________________________
3. Approaches Using LLMs
Below are detailed approaches to solve the problem using Large Language Models:
________________________________________
3.1 Single-Shot Prompting
Description:
For each invoice, dynamically generate a prompt with its fields and directly ask the LLM to predict the GL Code without any additional context.
Example Prompt:
You are a finance domain expert. Predict the GL Code for:

Description: Purchase of Office Chairs  
Business Type: Procurement  
Company Code: 1001  
Amount: 3000  
Tax Code: A0
Pros:
•	Fast to implement
•	Low complexity
•	Suitable for small-scale systems
Cons:
•	Lower accuracy
•	No memory of past examples
•	Sensitive to prompt quality
Best suited for: MVPs, PoCs, small datasets
________________________________________
3.2 Few-Shot Prompting
Description:
Provide a few manually curated examples of past invoices and their GL Codes within the prompt to guide the model before asking for a prediction.
Example Prompt:
Examples:
- Purchase of Office Chairs → GL Code: 620001
- Cloud Software Subscription → GL Code: 700123

Now predict for:
Description: Purchase of Laptops
Business Type: IT Support
Pros:
•	Improves prediction quality
•	No model training required
•	Flexible for moderate volume
Cons:
•	Prompt becomes large as examples increase
•	Hard to cover all edge cases
Best suited for: Moderate datasets where example variability matters
________________________________________
3.3 Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)
Description:
Build a vector database of historical invoice examples. For each new invoice, retrieve the top N similar invoices, then prompt the LLM with those examples to make an informed prediction.
Architecture:
1.	Embed past invoices into vectors.
2.	Perform similarity search for a new invoice.
3.	Construct the prompt using retrieved examples.
4.	Predict GL Code via LLM.
Pros:
•	Maintains historical memory
•	More accurate and context-aware
•	Handles large datasets well
Cons:
•	Needs separate vector DB setup
•	Slightly complex architecture
Best suited for: Production systems, medium-to-large datasets
________________________________________
3.4 Chain-of-Thought (CoT) Prompting
Description:
Instruct the LLM to reason step-by-step before predicting the GL Code, improving its logical consistency.
Example Prompt:
Let's reason step-by-step:
1. Identify nature of goods.
2. Match to correct business category.
3. Derive GL Code.

Invoice Details: ...
Pros:
•	Increases explainability
•	Reduces hallucinations
•	Useful for audit/compliance scenarios
Cons:
•	Longer and costlier prompts
•	Slower inference
Best suited for: Cases needing explainable AI outputs
________________________________________
3.5 Function Calling / Tool Use
Description:
Use OpenAI's function calling capabilities or similar APIs where the LLM can call backend functions like fetch_gl_code() to assist in GL Code mapping.
Pros:
•	Highly structured
•	Reduces hallucinations
•	Can integrate with enterprise master data systems
Cons:
•	Needs reliable GL mapping functions
•	Limited flexibility for unknown cases
Best suited for: Systems with relatively fixed GL mappings
________________________________________
3.6 Fine-Tuning Small LLMs
Description:
Fine-tune a small open-source LLM (e.g., Mistral 7B, Llama 3 8B) on historical invoice-to-GL code data to create a specialized GL Code predictor.
Steps:
•	Create instruction datasets.
•	Fine-tune using HuggingFace/OpenAI.
•	Deploy and serve as an API.
Pros:
•	Very fast inference
•	Customized for your business
•	No need for complex prompt engineering
Cons:
•	High upfront effort and cost
•	Needs large labeled datasets (1000+ samples)
Best suited for: High-scale production environments
________________________________________
3.7 Self-Consistency via Multiple Inference Calls
Description:
Send the same invoice to the LLM multiple times (e.g., 5 times) and use majority voting among the outputs to finalize the GL Code prediction.
Pros:
•	Reduces randomness
•	Improves stability of predictions
Cons:
•	3x–5x inference cost
•	Adds latency
Best suited for: High-accuracy critical systems
